    stem: "In Plato’s Republic, Socrates asks Cephalus whether or not old age is difficult.",
    questionText: "What reply does Cephalus give to Socrates?",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 20,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Cephalus says that people are wrong when they say that old age is a time of regrets and loss of pleasure and powers", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "He says that it is good to be free of “feelings of this sort” when your emotions are less intense, and you are no longer enslaved by them", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "He says that old age is easy to bear if you are sensible about it", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "Cephalus says that most of the old men he associates with are unhappy because they long for the lost pleasures of youth – making love, drinking, parties", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "They feel deprived and believe they are no longer living", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-6", kind: "point", verbatim: "They also complain that their families show no respect, and they harp on the miseries of old age", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-7", kind: "point", verbatim: "However, the father of Polemarchus claims that the fault lies not with old age, but in their characters which would also find youth a burden", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-8", kind: "point", verbatim: "By contrast, old age is peaceful for goodtempered, sensible old men such as Cephalus himself as they are glad to have left behind the tyranny of intense sexual desire, what the dramatist Sophocles describes as “so fierce and frenzied a master”", marks: null, openList: true },
    ],
